# Where the Black Ops II multiplayer weapon catalog comes from

Everything here was read on **2026-09-23**. `catalog.json` in this folder is the
list of every weapon a player can carry in Call of Duty: Black Ops II
**multiplayer** (the 2012 game, with its DLC); this file says where every fact
and every picture in it came from, and, because Activision's Black Ops II web
pages are mostly gone, exactly which facts no Activision page still confirms.

**Pictures: publisher pictures only (decision D116).** Every picture in
`weapons/` is Activision's own image. Activision's Black Ops II site is offline,
so each was downloaded from the Internet Archive's Wayback Machine copy of the
file at its original callofduty.com address; the Pictures table gives that
original address first and the archived copy after it. Eight of the nine are
cut from **Activision's own screenshots of the game's Create-a-Class screen**
(posted on the official Call of Duty community blog on 2012-12-13), so they are
the game's own weapon renders, exactly as the owner's screen draws them; the
ninth is the Peacekeeper art from Activision's Revolution DLC page. None came
from a fan wiki, Fandom, a loadout site or an image search. They are
Activision's copyright, used in the app to identify the weapon, the same risk
the owner accepted in D116. Delete a `.png` and set that weapon's `image` to
`null` and the app shows the class symbol instead; nothing else changes.

## What is in Black Ops II multiplayer

- **The 2012 game** (November 2012), with the launch arsenal below.
- **One DLC weapon: the Peacekeeper SMG.** The Revolution DLC (January 29, 2013
  on Xbox 360, February 28 on PlayStation 3 and PC) delivered "for the first
  time ever, a bonus multiplayer weapon, the 'Peacekeeper'", which Activision
  called "a one-time offering" (press releases below). The later DLC packs,
  Uprising, Vengeance and Apocalypse, added maps and Zombies content; their
  press releases name no multiplayer weapon (Vengeance's Ray Gun Mark II is a
  Zombies weapon).
- **Title Update 1.10 (September 10, 2026)** moved PlayStation multiplayer to
  dedicated servers and patched PC compatibility; press reports of its notes
  ([MP1st](https://mp1st.com/title-updates-and-patches/black-ops-2-update-1-10-1-010-released-september-10-includes-pc-version),
  [TheSixthAxis](https://www.thesixthaxis.com/2026/09/11/black-ops-2-now-has-dedicated-servers-on-ps4-ps5/))
  list no weapon change. No Activision page for it was found.
- **Plutonium T6**, the community PC client the owner plays on, was not
  checked: this is the game's own list. A modded server can add or change
  weapons.

Counts: **40 weapons in 8 classes**: Submachine Guns 7, Assault Rifles 9,
Shotguns 4, Light Machine Guns 4, Sniper Rifles 4, Pistols 5, Launchers 3,
Specials 4. 9 have an official picture.

**Not in the list, on purpose:** Scorestreak weapons (Death Machine, War
Machine), which come from a Scorestreak rather than a class; equipment (the
Combat Axe is a Lethal); the default melee attack, which is a button press,
not a weapon the player holds; and weapons found only in the campaign or in
Zombies.

## How the list is organised, and what is not confirmed

- **Class names.** Activision's own Peacekeeper FAQ says the Peacekeeper
  "resides in the Submachine Gun category"; the community references (below)
  write the classes "Submachine Guns", "Assault Rifles", "Shotguns", "Light
  Machine Guns", "Sniper Rifles", "Pistols", "Launchers" and "Special(s)", the
  full words rather than the "SMGs" and "LMGs" of later games. The in-game
  headings themselves were not seen on an official page.
- **Class order: Submachine Guns, Assault Rifles, Shotguns, Light Machine Guns,
  Sniper Rifles, then Pistols, Launchers, Specials.** Primary classes first,
  then secondary, as the game's Create-a-Class splits them. **Submachine Guns
  first is not confirmed** from any source: no page read gives Black Ops II's
  own menu order, and it is taken from the order Treyarch's other Black Ops
  games list their classes in. The owner's message listed Assault Rifles
  first; if his screen does, swap the first two groups (the ids do not change).
- **Specials.** In Create-a-Class the Assault Shield is the one primary
  Special, and the Crossbow and the Ballistic Knife are the secondary Specials;
  the game counts all of them, with the Combat Knife, as one class for the
  Specials' Diamond camo (community references below). They are one group
  here, the Assault Shield first, then the Crossbow and the Ballistic Knife in
  unlock order, then the Combat Knife.
- **The knife.** The only knife a player holds is the **Combat Knife**: a class
  with no primary and no secondary weapon runs with it out, and so does a
  player who plants the Assault Shield. It is not picked in Create-a-Class
  (Activision's own Create-a-Class screenshots only show a "Personalize Knife"
  button), so it is marked uncertain. The Ballistic Knife is a Special.
- **Order inside a class**: by the player level that unlocks each weapon
  (community references give the levels; the two weapons available from the
  start, level 4, in the order they list them), the Peacekeeper last because
  DLC. **The order on the in-game screen itself was not confirmed** from an
  official page.

## Names

**Black Ops II draws weapon names in mixed case**, not in capitals as Black
Ops 7 does: Activision's Create-a-Class screenshots show "Tac-45", "Mk 48" and
"Skorpion EVO". So here capitalisation is part of the name.

**Confirmed by Activision (10):**

- Read off the game's own Create-a-Class screen in Activision's screenshots
  (Pictures, below): **SWAT-556, SCAR-H, FHJ-18 AA, SMR, M27, Tac-45, Mk 48,
  Skorpion EVO**. The same blog post's text writes "Mk 48" and "Swat-556"; the
  screen writes SWAT-556, and the screen wins.
- **Peacekeeper**: the Revolution DLC page, both Revolution press releases and
  the Activision support FAQ.
- **Assault Shield**: the Black Ops II multiplayer features page ("defending
  your teammate with an assault shield", in running text).

**From community references only (30)**, because no Activision page that
still exists, or that the Wayback Machine kept, names them: MP7, PDW-57, Vector
K10, MSMC, Chicom CQB, MTAR, Type 25, FAL OSW, M8A1, AN-94, Remington 870 MCS, S12, KSG,
M1216, QBB LSW, LSAT, HAMR, SVU-AS, DSR 50, Ballista, XPR-50, Five-seven, B23R,
Executioner, KAP-40, SMAW, RPG, Crossbow, Ballistic Knife, Combat Knife. Each is
spelt as both the Call of Duty Wiki's Black Ops II weapon list and Gamer
Guides' Black Ops II weapon pages spell it (the Combat Knife, which Gamer
Guides does not list, as the wiki and the Steam guide do), except where they
